# Playtest notes / backlog

Open work only. When an item is done it **moves** to [`doc/dev/history.md`](doc/dev/history.md) — it does not get a checkmark and stay here, which is how this file grew to 33 completed entries and an item complaining about them.

Durable rationale ("why is the code like this?") belongs in [`doc/dev/decisions.md`](doc/dev/decisions.md); the chronological record, including approaches measured and reverted, is in `history.md`.

**Audited 2026-08-12** (the previous pass was 2026-08-10). Every open item making a checkable claim was re-verified by *running* something — a grep against `master`, a generated map, or the code itself — not by re-reading the item. That distinction is the point: several claims corrected below had been read past repeatedly, and three separate false claims were written into this file and into `decisions.md`/`history.md` in the two days before this audit.

**Corrected in this pass:**

- **Stage 6 was not open work.** It was built, measured at full protocol scale and reverted as a no-op — `decisions.md` had carried that verdict the whole time while this file described it as a stage still to build.
- **The lane hosts are not commented out.** `ssh-hosts.env` lists four, none commented; the MP-campaign item said the opposite. Heat is why that campaign is parked, not lanes. — **STALE WITHIN HOURS (noted 2026-08-15): `pc-tobi-laptop-guthand.lan` was commented out at 23:39 the same evening and still is.** Three lanes, not four. This line is left standing because it is what made the MP item's "worth investigating" bullet look credible for two days; probe the file, never a remembered count.
- **Player display names shipped** (2026-08-10, `getPlayerDisplayName` in `engine.ts`); the item still said no per-player identity concept existed. Removed.
- **`pickThreat` is at `combatPolicy.mjs:~980`**, not `~891` — the file grew today and every reference to that line was stale.
- **319 lines of finished multiplayer work (steps 1-7) moved to `history.md`**, where steps 8-11 already were. I moved 8-11 when they landed and left 1-7 behind, so the backlog carried them for three weeks.

**Verified still true**, so they need no re-checking: `pickThreat` filters `e.alive && e.aggroed`, and both gated and ranked on `Math.hypot` — confirming the walking-distance item, which was then *fixed* later the same day (see `history.md`); no threat give-up exists (reverted in `8390c8e`); the bot dispatches zero mouse events, so its aim is still keyboard-quantised; `VIRTUAL_STEP_MS` is still 50; `BOT_AIM_LEAD` is off (**no longer true — it ships ON gated at 2 tiles since 2026-08-14**) and `BOT_ANGULAR_FIRE_GATE` on; `BOT_WALKING_DISTANCE_BLOCKERS` defaults true; `DECORATIONS_ENABLED` is false; `hitFraction` is still `halfWidthPx / scatterPx`; `enemyAi.ts` still has exactly one `spawnProjectile` call site and no weapon concept; `HOST_NAV_DEADLINE_MS` and `perfDebug.ts` exist; the Firefox CI skip is still in place (4 sites).

Items recording a *measurement* rather than a code claim (the demo-campaign difficulty numbers, the damage-source breakdowns, the harness-specific wedge finding) were not re-run — they are history, and their dates say when they were true.

## Open

Grouped by area, and **within each group ordered by dependency** — the first item in a
section is the one whose answer changes the others, not the most urgent. Cross-section
dependencies are named in the items themselves. One-liners that have never been checked
against the code live in [Not yet thought through](#not-yet-thought-through) at the end.

### Bot & instrument

The bot is the measuring instrument for everything in the next section, so its defects are measurement defects. Ordered by dependency: nothing bot-shaped is worth scoping until the first item's A/B lands.

- [ ] **The bot is destined to become the deathmatch opponent, which changes what "good enough" means for every open bot item (user, 2026-08-09).** Recorded because it reorders the backlog rather than adding to it.
  - **The bar changes.** As an *instrument* the bot only has to be unbiased and stable — it may play badly as long as it plays badly *consistently*, which is why several defects were tolerable. As an *opponent* it has to be good and fun, and "consistently mediocre" is the failure mode rather than the escape hatch. `multiplayer-research.md` already files "adapt balancing AI for deathmatch/bots" as its own initiative and warns it is not an adaptation of existing code; this is the reason to believe that.
  - **Which open items get promoted.** They stop being measurement noise and become gameplay bugs the player would *see*:
    - **Threats have no give-up** — an opponent that locks onto something it cannot reach and stops playing is unmissable in PvP.
    - **`pickThreat` uses `visible` mostly as a sort key** — an opponent tracking you through a wall reads as cheating, which is worse than reading as stupid. **Narrowed 2026-08-12, not closed**: an occluded enemy is now filtered out when it is far *by corridor* (the walking-distance fix), so the blatant case is gone, but one occluded a few tiles away through a thin wall is still a live target and still tracked through it.
    - **The wedges** (curl 2.0% of level-visits, improved but unfixed) — a bot that stops moving is the single most visible failure a deathmatch opponent can have.
    - **Route/replan churn**, still unresolved.
  - **Three design choices are instrument-shaped and would be wrong for an opponent.** Worth knowing before anyone treats the current bot as a starting point:
    1. **Keyboard-only input.** `dispatchSegment` sends synthetic `KeyboardEvent`s and deliberately never the mouse. Aim is therefore quantised to key-hold durations — today's turn-split got the *granularity* right, but the mechanism is still "hold a turn key for N ms", not "point here". A credible deathmatch opponent wants direct aim.
    2. **A 50ms decision window** (`VIRTUAL_STEP_MS`), i.e. 20 decisions/sec, and `MultiplayerBot` raises `minDecisionMs` further for lockstep. Fine for measurement, coarse for duelling.
    3. **The profiles are bot-shaped, not player-shaped.** Casual/Gamer/Pro exist to span a *measurement* range (`fireAngleEps`, `ammoThrift`, `selfHarmAversion`); as difficulty tiers a player picks, they would want re-deriving from "how does this feel to fight" rather than "how does this spread telemetry".
       - **Measured evidence, 2026-08-21**: the balance pass (enemy HP 25 -> 35, Edge Cases 10-15 -> 25-35, trash healing cut) moved Gamer **+1%** and Pro **+3%** on the regenerated board while **Casual fell 71% and stopped finishing the campaign — 17 levels cleared before, 6 after**. Two profiles absorbed a difficulty increase that the third could not survive at all, which is the ladder failing in exactly the place a *player-shaped* Casual would need to work. Nothing caught it: Casual's board bar is "clear level 3", so a 6-level run qualified and was recorded as a pass, and the pre-merge smoke used Gamer and Pro — the two that turned out fine. **Read as a bot artifact, not a balance problem** (user's call, 2026-08-21): Casual has always sat too close to Gamer to be a real low-skill model, so what this exposed is the profile ladder rather than the game. Worth keeping because it is the first hard number on how compressed the ladder is under load.
  - **Today's aim fix is on this path**, not a detour: `turnSplitIntent` is the first change made because the bot should *play* better rather than because a metric was contaminated.
  - **The architectural groundwork is done and its throughput half is closed** — see `history.md` (2026-08-19). The bot and engine now share their tile/sight predicates, and the harness's throughput ceiling turned out to be one Node event loop, fixed by sharding lanes rather than by moving decisions into the page. What deathmatch still needs from that thread is the *in-page decision loop*, which was scoped but deliberately **not** built: it buys nothing for throughput now, and a shipped opponent cannot use `__codeensteinTestHooks` at all, since `isTestHooksActive()` is `import.meta.env.DEV`-gated and Vite strips the whole block from a production build. That gap, not threading, is what to scope first.

- [ ] **Bot: what the rework did *not* fix.** Two structural faults survived, both because the obvious fix was measured and made things worse:
    - **The grid-locked gait.** `driveToward` still aims at BFS tile centres a tile apart, and a turn still truncates the step issued with it. Giving each key its own hold duration was the planned fix; it cost 25pp of qualify rate on Pro/hard and raised level times on all three combos, because a short decision during heading correction *is* the control loop. Any retry has to keep re-evaluation frequent while turning. Symptom to watch: headed pure-turn share, measured 30% -> 44% after sprinting landed.
    - **It still concedes the first shot** (`pickThreat` filters `.aggroed`). Planned as its own stage and not attempted — the plan rates it the riskiest remaining change, since aggro is sticky and waking enemies that would have stayed asleep can't be undone within a level.
  Optional and unattempted: sprint-backpedal retreats instead of turning 180° and running blind (touches `criticalHealth`/`mineRetreat`, the branches the 0%->72% diagonal-strafe regression came from).

- [ ] **Bot mine retreat now backpedals instead of turning around — logic verified, real-world effect UNMEASURED (2026-07-31).** `mineRetreat` used to spin to face away from the mine while holding `KeyW`, so a retreat that began facing the mine (the normal case — you walk into one before noticing) *advanced on it* while turning: captured on `stage03_legacy_api.php` moving 7.86 -> 7.41, i.e. 0.45 tiles closer, mine 2.5 -> 2.2, over five decisions. That wasted motion is what made the retreat unable to finish: `findDangerousMine` fires at `MINE_BLAST_RADIUS + reactionBuffer` (2.4 + 0.32 = **2.72**) and `findDisarmableMine` only starts *above* it, but the bot never got past **2.6** before burning all `MINE_TARGET_GIVEUP_TICKS` (40 — exactly the count observed) and abandoning the mine. So it could neither pass nor shoot it. **The fix is the user's observation, not the original one:** the bot never needed to turn at all. `moveForward` takes a signed step (`forwardSign -= 1` for `KeyS`, engine.ts) so reversing is the same speed as advancing, sprint included — and backing up keeps the bot *facing* the mine, so the disarm shot is available the instant it clears the blast radius rather than requiring a second 180. **The bot had never dispatched `KeyS` anywhere in its history**; the whole backward axis was unused (it is Stage 7 of the rework plan, never built). `KeyS` also added to `TRANSLATING_KEYS` so a wedged backpedal is visible to `detectHeldKeyNoMovement`. **Caveat, stated plainly: unit-tested but never observed firing in play.** Across ~118,000 logged decisions in four runs (8 levels x 4, 3 levels x 3, 3 levels x 8), `mineRetreat` fired **zero** times, so `KeyS` never appeared. The original capture had 40 `mineRetreat` rows, so the branch does fire sometimes — the encounter is simply much rarer than that one capture suggested. Two earlier "it's fixed" readings in this session were invalid for exactly that reason: the post-fix runs had zero `mineRetreat` rows, so 0 oscillation findings measured the encounter not happening, not the fix working. **Do not A/B this until it has been seen firing** — the event is likely too rare to move any aggregate metric

- [ ] **All three notions of "level 1" now agree — the solver third was ALREADY FIXED (2026-08-07), and this item claimed otherwise for four days. Only a drift risk is left.** Verified 2026-08-11 by three checks: `order()` picks `sorted.find(withMain) ?? sorted[0]` (`stage-campaign.mjs:262`), which is exactly `findEntrypointByScanning`'s `bestWithMain ?? bestOverall`; `complexityByFile` scores with the browser's own `complexityScore` and the zero-complexity filter uses that same number; and the `findEntrypointByName` stage is unreachable for a staged campaign *by construction*, since staging renames every file `${slot}_${path}` and `03_src_main.c` never equals `main.c`. The "models only the `bestOverall` branch" claim described the pre-2026-08-07 world; I repeated it without checking. The rest of the item has moved to `history.md`.
  - **The residual is a drift risk, not a bug.** The solver *re-derives* the rule where the planner now *asks* for it (`campaignLevelOrder`). It agrees today; it can silently stop agreeing if `findEntrypointByScanning` or the staging naming scheme changes, and nothing would fail. Making `order()` ask too would close that — worth doing only if the staging path misbehaves again.

### Balancing, measurement & capture tooling

Ordered so the instrument comes before what it measures: the first item is about ghidra's damage model and its telemetry being wrong, and a capture booked on either would measure the instrument rather than the balance. Of the three below, only the difficulty one needs no machine time at all — it is answered offline by the solver, and it says what a capture would and would not add.

- [ ] **Ghidra's damage model and its telemetry are both wrong, and neither of them is the tunnelling bug (measured 2026-08-19, desk pass, no capture).** This replaces the "re-check every rocket/ghidra conclusion" item that stood here. That re-check is **done and the answer is that nothing needs re-measuring** — the numbers are in `history.md`. Its own "cheap first pass" bullet had the mechanism backwards, which is the reason to read the closure rather than trust the summary. What the pass turned up instead is two live defects, a live blind spot, and a better question.
  - **The scorer says 150, the engine delivers ~131.** `expectedDamagePerShot` returns a flat `damagePerPellet` for ghidra everywhere inside ~10 tiles, i.e. exactly the band the bot fires rockets in (measured p10/p50/p90 = 5.0/6.0/7.3 tiles). But `ROCKET_ENEMY_TRIGGER_RADIUS` is 0.4, so a *direct hit* detonates up to 0.4 tiles off the target's centre and `rocketDamageAt` then applies blast falloff from there — simulated **~131 HP** to the primary target, near-invariant across profile, archetype, range and geometry. So the model is **~13% optimistic per rocket even after the fix**. Pointed out rather than fixed: it changes weapon choice, so it wants its own A/B and it moves no metric until the bot fires more than 0.105% of its shots as rockets.
  - **The same function also under-values ghidra past ~10 tiles, for a reason that does not exist.** `expectedDamagePerShot` applies `widthOverScatter` to every single-pellet weapon, discounting ghidra to 0.69/0.49/0.35/0.26 of its damage at 11/12/13/14 tiles. A rocket has no cone: `fire()` hits `if (w.isRocket) { spawnRocket(...); return; }` and never reaches `resolveShot`, so it flies exactly along `dirX/dirY`. Harmless today only because the archive contains **2 rocket shots beyond 10 tiles out of 3,466**.
  - **`report:damage-model`'s ghidra rows measure nothing at all** — not "measured through a bug", *nothing*. It attributes damage by joining `damageDealt` to a `shot` on a shared timestamp, and a rocket's damage lands seconds later, at the far end of its flight. Run over the whole archive it reports ghidra at **100% miss and ~0.0 damage/shot** in every range bucket. So "anything tuned off its rocket rows" is moot; there was never a number there to tune off.
    - **Its own header doc's "a 2026-08-06 capture measured ghidra 93 against 150" does not reproduce** from the captures of that date or any other — the script prints `100% miss / 0.0` for them. The shotgun half of that same sentence does roughly reproduce. The claim is left standing in the doc with the retraction appended next to it rather than deleted, because whatever produced 93 is still unaccounted for.
  - **Rockets were invisible to the hit-side telemetry entirely** — **0** of 3,248,140 `hit` events across the archive are ghidra, for the same `return`-before-`resolveShot` reason. **CLOSED 2026-08-20: the second of the two options named here is built.** `rocketDetonated` (schema 3) records `direct`, `dist`, `enemiesHit`, `dmg`, `selfDmg` and a `hits` array in the same `{eid, arch, amt}[]` shape `damageTaken.by` uses, so "how many enemies did one shot hit" is now the same query for a rocket as for a shotgun blast. `direct` is the part that was unrecoverable: splash lands whether an enemy or the wall stopped the rocket, so damage alone cannot tell them apart — the 2026-08-19 tunnelling analysis had to simulate exactly that distinction. **Captures taken before this date still cannot answer any of it**, so the archive stays blind; the next capture is the first that will not be.
  - **A smaller live mismatch, recorded because it is invisible until Elites are fought at range.** The fire gate is `min(fireAngleEps, angularHalfWidth(dist, target))`, which scales with the target's sprite; the rocket's trigger radius is a fixed 0.4 tiles. An Elite projects 1.5x, so from **8 tiles out the gate passes an aim error of 0.433 tiles that the trigger radius will not catch**. The rocket still splashes, so it is a damage haircut and not a whiff. Zero archived shots hit this case.
  - **ANSWERED 2026-08-20, offline, no machine time. The bot declines it because `scoreRangedWeapon` essentially never picks it — and neither the ghidra nor the shotgun question is a scoring-tuning problem at all.**
    - **Both weapons are chosen almost entirely by the cluster fast-path at the top of `pickRangedWeapon`, which bypasses scoring completely.** On `clusterCount >= 2` it hands out Friday Hotfix inside `FRIDAY_HOTFIX_FULL_DAMAGE_RANGE` (2.5), then ghidra beyond `ROCKET_CLUSTER_MIN_DIST` (5) if `!threat.edgeCase`, then **falls through to the shotgun unconditionally — no distance test, no HP test, no archetype test**.
    - **The observed distances are the proof, and they line up on the constants to within a tile.** Friday p50 **2.8** (gate 2.5); shotgun p50 **3.0** — exactly the band Friday stops claiming; ghidra p10 **5.1**, p50 5.9 (gate 5.0), i.e. *essentially every rocket the bot has ever fired came through that one branch*.
    - **The scorer, by contrast, picks the shotgun in exactly one cell of the whole grid**: target HP ~150 *and* distance <=1.5. Swept HP 15-300 x distance 0.5-6 and it wins nowhere else. Only **2.5%** of real shotgun shots were inside 1.5 tiles (p50 3.0) and **9%** at HP 135-180 — so the scorer explains almost none of its use.
    - **Why the scorer refuses it**: the pistol fires at 0.16s against the shotgun's 0.85s, so an 8x damage advantage loses to a 5.3x cadence disadvantage the moment `Math.ceil` demands a second shell — and `magazineSize: 2` then charges a full 1.2s reload for that second shell. At 2 tiles: shotgun 3.70s against pistol 1.54s.
    - **The reload charge is deliberate** ("Ignoring it would leave the scorer preferring the shotgun and ghidra by roughly a reload each") but it is worth knowing what it costs. Charging only the reloads needed *before* the killing shot leaves the shotgun still losing (2.50 vs 1.54) — but flips ghidra outright: **2.95s -> 1.35s against the pistol's 1.54s**. So for ghidra that single term is the decision.
    - **The asymmetry to look at first**: in the cluster path ghidra is barred whenever the threat is an Edge Case, and the shotgun is barred by nothing — so the shotgun inherits every cluster ghidra is refused. Edge Cases are **62% of all spawns**. That is the mechanism behind `eventMetrics.mjs`'s standing suspicion, now confirmed from the constants and the shot distribution rather than guessed.
    - **Consequence for anyone tuning this**: retuning `scoreRangedWeapon` will barely move either weapon's usage, because it is not what selects them. The lever is the fast-path's structure. Two null ghidra A/Bs were designed off a synthetic `pickRangedWeapon` probe and cost ~55 min each; this is why they were null.
    - **FOLLOW-UP 2026-08-20: the fast-path's premise checks out, its close-range handoff does not.** `resolveShot` resolves a target *per pellet*, and measured over 187k shots the shotgun hits 2+ enemies on **23.8%** of its shots against **0.0%** for pistol and gdb — so it really is the only spread weapon, and the fast-path is covering a blind spot the single-target scorer cannot express. But Friday Hotfix, which is handed every cluster inside 2.5 tiles, multi-hits **8.3%** in that band against the shotgun's **17.5%**, and **0.3% beyond it** — it is not a spread weapon in practice, only the fastest killer. Full tables in `history.md`.
    - **Still unmeasurable, and it blocks the other half**: ghidra's 2.6-tile splash is invisible here because rockets emit no `hit` events (0 of 3.2M). Any change to the ghidra side of the fast-path is unguided until a detonation event exists.

- [ ] **Difficulty is inconsistent along the campaign, not across repos. Drift is MEASURED and settled; jaggedness is open and no constant tested so far moves it (2026-08-21, offline, no machine time).** Started from "1.0 soon but balance feels too easy sometimes and too hard elsewhere — should we capture fresh data?" The answer was no: the offline solver answers it in minutes, and the stored captures all predate beta-8 anyway. Full numbers in `history.md`.
  - **Two axes, not one, and they have different causes.** Over 7,088 fought levels across all 23 corpus repos on `hard`:
    - *Drift* — a campaign trivialises itself. Median clear ratio without farming: **9.6 at level 1, 19.8 at 4-8, 104 at 21-50, 4,548 past level 200**, while the roster stays flat (7 enemies at level 1, 14 at 21-50, 8 past 200). Carryover compounds, the opposition does not.
    - *Jaggedness* — neighbours swing wildly. Median |log| step between consecutive levels is **3.28x**, and sinatra is the shape of it: level 5 is `base.rb` at 462 enemies, level 7 is `main.rb` at 3.
  - **Capping carryover fixes drift and does nothing for jaggedness.** `--carryover-cap K` (in the solver only, see below) at K=3: per-repo drift **22.2x -> 1.3x**, deepest-bucket median **4,548 -> 48**. Jaggedness **3.28x -> 2.92x**, i.e. untouched. So the cap is a real lever for one axis and irrelevant to the other.
  - **CORRECTED 2026-08-21 — the "roster outlier" reading was wrong, and it was wrong because it was never traced.** The claim was that the five levels a K=3 cap makes unclearable are huge-roster vim files. Tracing `vim` lv165 showed the real shape: under a cap the non-bullet pools decay to **zero and never recover**, because `carryForward` banks every drop as bullets — a simplification whose own comment calls it "immaterial", and which *is* immaterial only while carryover is unbounded. So part of that result was a model artifact, not a game property. What survives the correction is a better metric, below.
  - **STAGE 0b DONE. The right metric is self-funding = (pre-placed + own drops) / total HP** — carryover-independent, so no cap needed to read it. Over the same 7,088 levels: median **4.28**, p10 1.73, and **181 levels (2.6%) cannot fund themselves**. It does *not* drift with position (2.84 at level 1, 4.41 past 200), which confirms drift is purely carryover.
    - **The mechanism is HP-per-enemy, not roster size.** Median HP/enemy by self-funding band: **25** where healthy (>3x), 62, 121, and **162** in the worst band. Elite share is ~0% in every band — 0 of the 181 failures are Elite-dominated and 71 have no Elite at all. The worst self-funders are often *tiny*: `vim` lv9 is **2 enemies, 300 hp**; `codeenstein3d` lv19 is **1 enemy, 150 hp**. Loot is per *kill* (`BULLETS_DROP_AMOUNT` 4, flat, whatever died) while cost is per *HP*, so a file whose complexity sits in few big functions cannot pay for itself.
    - **`COMPLEXITY_PER_EXTRA_ENEMY` is a real lever on that tail** (swept 5/3/2 against the live generator): unfundable levels **181 -> 66 -> 36**, p10 self-funding 1.73 -> 2.05 -> 2.51. It works by splitting HP across more bodies, i.e. more drop rolls. Median level barely moves (9 -> 11 enemies), so this hits outliers and leaves typical levels alone. Cost is the documented one: it re-rolls every layout, invalidates every replay, needs a `defaultHighscore.ts` regeneration.
    - **Nothing tested so far moves jaggedness.** Clear-ratio swing between adjacent levels: 3.28x uncapped, 3.15x at per=2, 2.92x with a K=3 carryover cap. It is file-size variation, which "a file is a level" makes structural. If adjacent-level consistency matters for 1.0, the lever is campaign *ordering* or a per-level normalisation, not a balance constant.
    - **PRICED 2026-08-21: HP-scaled drops work, but only budget-neutral, and they do not touch jaggedness.** Scaling a regular kill's ammo drop by `maxHp / ref` (`balancing:budget --hp-scaled-drops <ref>`, Elites excluded). At `ref` 59 — the corpus *mean* HP/enemy, so budget-neutral — corpus drop damage moves **+0.8%**, unfundable levels fall **181 -> 21**, median clear ratio tightens 19%, and nothing is pushed below clearable. At `ref` 33 (the *median*) it inflates the corpus economy **+80%** and the median clear ratio **+43%**: preserving the median level does not preserve the total when the tail is this heavy. The 21 residuals are tiny early levels (doom lv2 is 2 enemies, 41 hp) where the metric is meaningless — read self-funding against absolute HP.
    - **Shipping cost, and it is mislocated:** `simulationBalanceCoverage.test.ts` covers `combatConstants.ts` and `traps.ts` — **`loot.ts` is not in the balance hash**. Changing drop amounts leaves `balanceHash` unmoved, so every stored replay validates and then diverges in ammo, the same door the 2026-08-19 rocket fix went through. Either fold the loot constants into `SIMULATION_BALANCE` (hash moves, replays invalidated, `defaultHighscore.ts` regeneration) or ship a silent replay break.
    - **Jaggedness is a robust negative across three levers now**: 3.28x shipped, 2.92x with a K=3 carryover cap, 3.15x at `COMPLEXITY_PER_EXTRA_ENEMY` 2, 3.28x with HP-scaled drops. It is not reachable from the balance constants. Next attempt should be campaign *ordering* or a per-level normalisation, not a fourth knob.
  - **Do not book a bot A/B for the cap.** The drift half is settled on 7,088 levels and a capture adds nothing to it. What a capture *could* add and the solver cannot is **deaths** — the solver is a resource model with a flat survival constant (4.0s vs one regular, 1.3s vs three, 2.0s vs an Elite, identical on every repo, because every combat stat but HP is shared). That is a lethality question and wants its own design; the A/B recipe's four combos and paired seeds still apply when it does.
  - **Why the knob is not in `engine.ts`.** `capCarryover` lives in `scripts/lib/levelSolver.mjs`, default `Infinity` = shipped behaviour, reached by `balancing:budget --carryover-cap <n>`. Putting the constant in the engine means folding it into `SIMULATION_BALANCE`, which moves `balanceHash`, invalidates every shipped replay and costs a `defaultHighscore.ts` regeneration — not a price to pay for a measurement that might have said the lever does not work. Pay it when a lever is chosen, not while pricing one.
  - **State:** the two solver bugs this work uncovered — the NaN drop budget and the missing TTK reload term — **shipped in PR #133**. What is still unlanded is the pricing work itself: four commits on `fix/solver-shells-drop-budget` (unpushed, rebased onto master after #133), holding the two experiment knobs (`--carryover-cap`, `--hp-scaled-drops`), the sweeps, and the write-ups. Nothing in them changes game behaviour — both knobs default to the shipped values — so they can wait for a decision on which lever to actually build.
  - **The playtest bot cannot measure the health economy at all — do not book an A/B on it (measured 2026-08-21).** A 10-attempt smoke on `demo-campaign` at Gamer/normal and Pro/hard, 6 levels each, qualified **10/10 on both** with zero deaths — but the reason is that the bot takes **~17 damage per level against a 100-point bar** (100 total over six levels on normal, 144 on hard) and therefore sits at full health nearly always. The guaranteed heal only spawns `if (shooter.health < MAX_HEALTH)`, so `healingBySource` reads **`pickupHealth: 0`** on every level of both arms; the only healing recorded at all is a single point of melee lifesteal. `HEALTH_DROP_AMOUNT`, `HEALTH_SCALE_REFERENCE_HP` and any future health lever are invisible to this instrument — an A/B would return a confident null, the same shape as the wedge fix measured in a harness that could not wedge. Health questions are settled by play, or by a bot that can be made to take damage, and not otherwise. It also means the smoke's 10/10 says only *the regeneration will not grind*, not that the balance is unchanged.
  - **CAVEAT on every "level N" number in this item (found 2026-08-21): `report-level-budget.mjs` is not numbering levels the way the game does.** It walks the file tree from index 0; the game starts at `findEntrypoint`'s pick and walks *forward* from there (`advanceToNextLevel` -> `findNextParsableFile`), so its level 1 is the entrypoint, not the alphabetically-first file. `stage-campaign.mjs`'s `order()` models this correctly and the budget tool never did. **Order-independent findings are unaffected** — HP distributions, one-shot rates, roster sizes, self-funding, health per kill, which is everything the shipped enemy-HP change rests on. **Position-framed findings are not**: drift by campaign position, "the first 20 levels", clear-ratio-by-position and the whole carryover analysis are computed over a different ordering than a player sees. The mechanism survives (carryover compounds over however many levels are played while the roster does not scale); the bucket boundaries do not. Surfaced when a playtest report could not be matched to the levels this tool named. Fixing the tool is small — reuse `order()` — and worth doing before any position-based conclusion is acted on.
  - **PARKED 2026-08-21 by the user, pending manual playtests — and parked at the right moment.** Every number above comes from a *resource* model: can this level be cleared with the ammo available. The solver has no death model at all (survival is a flat constant, identical on every repo, because every combat stat but HP is shared), so if "too hard" means *dying* rather than running dry, nothing measured here speaks to it. Play is the only instrument that does.
  - **What would be most useful to write down while playing**, because it is what the offline work cannot supply:
    - *Which level number* a run died on, and on which repo — the whole finding is that difficulty tracks campaign position, so a death at level 5 and a death at level 40 mean opposite things.
    - *Whether it was ammo or damage.* Dry when you died, or overwhelmed with ammo in reserve? The measured answer is that ammo is comfortable everywhere in the first 20 levels (worst clear ratio 2.07x, median ~20x), so a death for lack of ammo would contradict the model and is the single most valuable observation available.
    - *Roster size at the moment it felt wrong.* First-20 rosters run median 10, p90 85, max 481 — a 60x swing with no clamp. If "too hard" correlates with a big room count rather than with tough individuals, the untested roster cap is the lever and the three priced ones are beside the point.
    - *Where a campaign stopped being interesting*, as a level number. Drift says the median level is 5x easier by level 20 and 100x by level 50; a subjective boredom point would tell us which of those matters.

- [ ] **The shells/reload economy: the A/B is REFUTED before running it — the bot is not starving on shells (measured 2026-08-20, offline, no machine time).** Magazines and the shotgun's own `shells` pool shipped 2026-08-15 with every number sized off what the shared pool used to afford, not off a capture: `STARTING_SHELLS` 12, `SHELLS_DROP_AMOUNT` 2, `ELITE_SHELLS_DROP_AMOUNT` 8, the 70/30 loot-weight split, four `reloadSec` values. This item proposed answering *"is the bot starving on shells"* first, with an 80-attempt arm. That question is now answered from event logs the density capture already wrote — **2,609 level-starts across 240 attempts on staged curl**:
  - mean shells **at level start: 13.9**, i.e. *above* the starting 12 — the pool accumulates rather than depletes. Carried into a level: p10=7, p50=10, p90=27, max 60.
  - **1.6%** of level-starts have zero shells; **0.91%** of shotgun shots empty the pool; only **5.0%** leave <=2.
  - the shotgun is **2.9% of all shots** (6,238 of 212,210).
  - **So the economy constraint does not bind, and an A/B on these numbers would tune a resource the bot barely touches** — the same availability-before-efficacy shape as the ghidra finding. Do not book the night run this item asked for.
  - **The live question it exposes instead is why the shotgun is 2.9% of shots at all** — a `scoreRangedWeapon` question answerable offline against the existing corpus, exactly like the ghidra one. If that changes and the bot starts using the shotgun, re-open this item: the numbers are still unmeasured, they just cannot be measured through an instrument that does not fire the weapon.

- [ ] balancing
    - [ ] `npm run balancing:telemetry` (`scripts/run-balancing-telemetry.mjs`) now exists: a headless-Chromium bot plays `demo-campaign` with 3 profiles (Casual/Gamer/Pro) across all 3 difficulties (9 combos), collecting 3 qualifying runs each (a run "qualifies" once it clears level 3), and dumps a 7-category telemetry breakdown per level + campaign aggregate to `balancing_telemetry.json` (gitignored, regenerate on demand). Driven by new `window.__codeensteinTestHooks` hooks (`getTelemetrySnapshot()`, `getMines()`) and engine-side tracking in `src/engine/telemetry.ts`. A first round of balance tuning from this data has already shipped (loot drop-rate/amount reduction + a Toolchain miss-chance unlock, an Easy-difficulty damage increase, and worse enemy aim on Easy/Normal — see CHANGELOG's Unreleased section) — kept open for further iteration as more telemetry accumulates.
    - [ ] `npm run balancing:telemetry-multiplayer` (`scripts/run-balancing-telemetry-multiplayer.mjs`) enhancements — raised 2026-07-22 while a full default sweep (all player counts/difficulties/profiles + mixed combos) was live for several hours:
        - [ ] **Casual/Hard is close to a pointless combo.** Live data: `Casual/hard/2p` needed 30+ attempts to land just its 2nd qualifying run (a well under 10% qualifying rate) — the weakest profile at the hardest difficulty has a near-nonexistent real win chance, at least for a full/strict qualifying bar. Either exclude Casual from the Hard difficulty tier in the default matrix, or give it a much smaller qualifying target / explicit attempt cap so one unlucky combo can't blow up the whole sweep's wall-clock cost the way it did here.
        - [ ] **Qualifying bar could be more lenient than "fully clear the level."** This script already only drives one bundled level per run, not a full campaign (see its own top doc comment) — but if a future pass ever extends it toward multi-level/full-campaign coverage, don't require a full campaign clear to "qualify": mirror single-player's own per-profile qualify levels (`generate-default-highscore.mjs`'s `QUALIFY_LEVEL_INDEX_BY_PROFILE`, e.g. reaching level 4) rather than demanding full completion, especially given how brutal full clears are proving for weak-profile/hard-difficulty combos above.

- [ ] **demo-campaign measured with a correct bot (2026-08-09): 14 of its 17 levels are free, and L15 is the entire campaign. Measurement only — no content changed.**
  - **SUPERSEDED 2026-08-12: the campaign no longer kills the bot at all, and L15 is not the wall any more.** Measured while A/B'ing the pellet-cone model at `LEVEL_LIMIT=17`: **160 attempts across 8 combos (4 profile x difficulty cells x 2 arms), zero failures**, with 19-20 of 20 reaching **both** L15 and L17 in every cell. Pro/hard specifically went from the 1-of-42 clearing L15 recorded above to **20 of 20**.
  - **What changed is the bot, not the content** — nothing in `demo-campaign/` moved between the two measurements, while the loot treadmill, the standoff, the Elite cap, the angular fire gate, the level-1 planner desync and the teleporter-pad give-up all did. The 2026-08-09 numbers are not wrong; they describe a bot that no longer exists.
  - **The consequence is bigger than this item.** The demo campaign is now exhausted as an A/B substrate: with a 100% clear rate and zero deaths, `qualifyRate`, the conditional death rate and attrition **cannot discriminate any bot change**, at `LEVEL_LIMIT=8` or 17. Yesterday I blamed the qualify-bar blind spot for that; the real reason is simply that there is nothing left to survive. **Any future bot A/B needs a staged real repo** (ripgrep/curl/serilog) rather than this campaign, or its survival half is decoration.
    - **RESOLVED 2026-08-13 — the substrate is staged curl, and the recipe is in `history.md`.** Piloted live rather than picked from the archive, because every per-repo number on record was stale: L1-L8 clear at 100%, **L9 at 45%, L10 at 33%**, nothing past L11. Use per-level clear rate at L9/L10 and levels-cleared-per-attempt; **campaign completion is 0% and must not be a metric** — it is saturated at the floor exactly as demo-campaign is at the ceiling. wolf3d also discriminates but costs ~7x (0.29 vs 1.96 attempts/min) and was dropped. Two cautions: re-staging curl does **not** reproduce the 2026-08-09 staging (different files at slots 7/10/12/14), and this harness produced a **20.4pp swing at n~20 from a lever measured flat**, so treat anything under ~25pp at that n as noise.
  - **One unexplained detail, recorded rather than rounded away**: two of the eight cells show `sampleCount` 19 rather than 20 from L15 on, with `failures=0` and no stuck or crash. A run stopped contributing snapshots without dying. Same shape as the single missing run seen on 2026-08-11. Not chased.
  - **Hard, 45 runs, bot behaving correctly** (the give-up A/B's OFF arm, which is what HEAD now does):

    ```
    L1-L9 45/45   L10 44/45   L11 43/44   L12 43/43   L13 43/43   L14 42/43
    L15 1/42      L16 1/1     L17 1/1
    ```

    Three deaths across levels 1-14 combined, then **L15 kills 41 of 42**. L16 and L17 clear whenever they are reached. So the campaign's difficulty is not spread thin — it is one level, and everything after it is a formality.
  - **This is the same shape the old bot showed** (2026-08-05: L1-14 flawless 16/16, L15 8/16, L17 0/8), so it is not an artefact of this bot. What changed is that L17 is now clearable — the Elite cap turned its 3 Elites from ~3,225 HP each into packs of ~500, and total room HP is essentially untouched (9,665 vs 9,675). The wall moved from "L15 *and* L17" to "L15 alone".
  - **The premise of the question holds, with a caveat.** The campaign was tuned against a bot with at least four defects, and `ELITE_HP_MULTIPLIER` was halved 4->2 specifically because the bot died 12/12 on level 12 — a fight it was knife-trading. But *this* measurement does not rest on that: 14 levels at ~99% clear on Hard is the finding, and it reproduces across two very different bots.
  - **What "harder" has to mean now.** Elites are capped, so a single huge function can no longer make a wall; the measured difficulty driver is exposure-time x enemy count (three A/Bs, total damage taken invariant at 122/127/126). Harder levels therefore mean source files with **more callable entities**, not one monster function. And L1-14 being gentle may be deliberate — this is the showcase campaign — so the case is strongest for the back half.
  - **Easy/normal numbers from the same session are CONTAMINATED** and must not be quoted: they come from the full 3-difficulty capture, which ran with the give-up active (reverted in `8390c8e` as a regression). Only the hard arm above is clean. Re-measure easy/normal before using them.
  - **The bot is not a difficulty oracle.** It is superhuman at some things and far worse at others — measured taking up to 53% of its damage while not fighting. "The bot clears it" is evidence the content is unchallenging *to this instrument*, not that a player will breeze it. What makes the case here is the margin (3 deaths in 14 levels x 45 runs), not the bare clear rate.
  - **Sequencing trap**: `defaultHighscore.ts` is generated from this campaign, so any content change invalidates it. Regenerate *after* deciding difficulty, not before — this session regenerated twice for exactly this reason.

- [ ] **Regular and Edge Case enemies do 75-100% of the damage and have no backlog item (2026-08-06).** Written in place of the refuted "level size is the real outlier" bullet. Across the 7-repo sweep, pooling raw `damageTaken.by`: Elites are **at most a quarter** of all damage a run takes (wolf3d 24.8%, codeenstein3d 21.3%, laravel 10.4%, curl 10.0%, ripgrep and the two zero-Elite repos 0%), while regular + Edge Case do the rest. Elites dominate only the *final* level (62-98%) — they are the full stop, not the attrition.
  - **ripgrep is the pure case**: 0% Elite damage, its single Elite never reached, and it still lost every one of 45 runs. An Elite clamp would do nothing for it.
  - **Deliberately not a proposed fix.** This is a hypothesis about where to look, not a measured problem with a known size — and per `remediation-order-2026-08-06.md` it should not be sized until the bot fixes land, since the current numbers are contaminated by the bot walking 14,000 tiles for loot and knife-trading Elites.
  - **What would size it**: after Stage C's re-measure, the share of deaths where no Elite was present at all, and the damage-per-enemy the attrition archetypes actually deliver. Review §10.
  - **SIZED 2026-08-13 on ~1,000 post-fix runs, and the hypothesis holds — but the interesting number is not the one this item asked for.** Full tables in `history.md`. Attrition archetypes do **88.4%** of enemy damage on curl (the only one of the three staged repos that contains Elites at all) and **100%** on serilog and ripgrep, which stage none. The Elite share on curl is **11.6%**, against **10.0%** in the pre-fix sweep above — so the contamination this item was waiting out barely moved this particular metric, and the hypothesis survives its own re-measure.
  - **Per-enemy, Elites are ~5x deadlier and still irrelevant**: curl damage-per-spawn is Elite **17.4**, normal **3.6**, Edge Case **0.7** — but 1,536 Elites against 46,309 normals and 49,151 Edge Cases. Individually lethal, numerically a rounding error. That is the mechanism behind "the full stop, not the attrition", now measured rather than inferred.
  - **The "deaths with no Elite present" metric turned out uninformative, and that is worth recording so nobody re-runs it.** On curl 99.5% of deaths happen on a level whose roster contains an Elite — but so does nearly every curl level, so the number measures staging, not lethality. Damage share is the discriminating quantity; roster presence is not.
  - **New and unasked-for: environmental damage varies enormously by repo** — 5.0% on curl, 7.6% on serilog, **29.7% on ripgrep** (mines 16.3% + acid 12.1%). That is a third of all damage taken coming from level furniture rather than enemies, on the repo whose Casual arm clears 3%. Anyone looking at ripgrep difficulty should start there rather than at its roster.

