### Map generation & gameplay content

Two dependency chains: AST facts unlock the enemy archetypes, and the decoration mechanism (a tile kind plus a texture slot) is shared by the three wall items that follow it.

- [ ] **Richer per-entity AST facts: recursion, call-graph fan-in, async, deprecation. MEASURED 2026-08-20 and it does NOT work as a route to enemy archetypes — do not build it for that.** Split out 2026-08-19 from the enemy-diversity item, which wants them for archetypes. A prevalence check over **7,101 callable entities** across 8 repos in 8 languages, run before building anything, says three of the four barely fire and the fourth cannot be approximated:
  - **Prevalence, overall**: recursion **10.2%**, async **1.5%**, deprecation **0.2%**. For scale, the existing facts this was meant to improve on are private/protected 33.8%, `nestingDepth>=2` 14.9%, `switchBranches` 4.7%. So async and deprecation are *worse than the worst fact already available*, and the item ranks them "Tier 1, nearly free" — the cost ranking is inverted against the value.
  - **The one that looked transformative is invalid.** The fan-in proxy the item itself proposes ("a workspace-wide identifier-occurrence count") reads 50.2% at >=10 occurrences — but it is measuring **name commonality, not call-graph position**: mean name length 7.8 chars in the high group against 14.8 in the low, corr(name length, count) **-0.370**, and the top "hubs" are `args` and `path`, the latter appearing 7 times because every `path` *variable* in ripgrep counts.
  - **Restricting to call sites (`name(`) does not rescue it**: corr stays **-0.300**, name length 8.2 vs 14.0, and the language spread runs 12.5% (django) to 72.3% (serilog) — the same language-marker problem private/protected has. Real cross-file resolution is the item's own "not a small change".
  - **The number that decides it**: of the **51.2%** of entities carrying no existing fact, recursion+async+deprecation together reach **12.3%** of them. The factless share would go from 51.2% to ~44.9%. That is not what unblocks a fourth archetype.
  - **Caveat, stated so the verdict can be re-tested**: these are text probes over each entity's source span, not AST extraction. But they closely match the *proposed* implementations — the item itself specifies same-name matching for recursion and an identifier count for fan-in — and deprecation at 0.2% would survive a 5x undercount.
  - **So the conclusion is about the whole approach, not these four facts**: enemy archetype variety cannot be driven by per-entity AST facts, because every candidate is rare, language-bound, or not cheaply resolvable. **The facts with full coverage and no language skew are the ones already extracted** — `complexityScore` and the line span. If a fourth archetype is wanted, its assignment rule should key off those, or off room role/position, not off new parsing.
  - **Terrain is a separate question and this does not answer it.** The item notes terrain could use these too; at 1.5% and 0.2% that would be flavour on a handful of rooms, which may still be worth it on its own merits. It just is not an archetype lever.
  - **Tier 1, nearly free — async and deprecation.** `vocabulary.ts` is *one flat merged table* of Tree-sitter node type -> meaning with **no per-language branching**: `descendantsOfType()` on a type a grammar doesn't have simply returns nothing, so one table runs against every language. A syntactic marker (an `async` modifier, an annotation/attribute/decorator node) is rows in that table, plus an optional `refinements.ts` hook for languages that need shape — and those hooks are explicitly "additive: a language with no entry gets the shared default", so this can land language by language instead of all at once.
  - **Tier 2, one traversal — recursion.** Call-expression nodes are table-driven, but deciding "does this call target the entity it sits inside" is resolution logic rather than vocabulary. Same-name matching is a decent heuristic and cheap; overloads, aliases, method dispatch and higher-order calls make it approximate. **Decide up front that approximate is acceptable** — it is, for choosing an enemy archetype.
  - **Tier 3, a different problem — call-graph fan-in.** "How many entities call this one" needs *cross-file* name resolution: imports, namespaces, overloads, dynamic dispatch. That is not a vocabulary entry and not a small change. **The cheap substitute is a workspace-wide identifier-occurrence count** — language-agnostic, wrong in the ways you would expect, and almost certainly good enough to pick a "hub" archetype. Reach for that before building a resolver.
  - **Plus the two bespoke adapters.** PHP and C are imperative (`phpParser.ts`, `cParser.ts`) because their grammars need it, so each new fact needs matching hand-written extraction there — 2 files, not 15, but they will not come for free from the table.
  - **THE cost, and it is invisible from the idea's wording: `astHash` is `hashRun(JSON.stringify(parsed), campaignName)`.** Any new field on `CodeEntity` changes *every* AST hash, which makes every shipped replay in `defaultHighscore.ts` and every stored highscore entry stale — playback compares and refuses. **So batch all four facts into one change and regenerate the board once, afterwards.** Landing them one at a time costs a full `generate:default-highscore` run each (~33 min), and the sequencing trap is already documented: regenerate *after* the design settles, never before.
  - **One design question to settle before extracting anything**: "async" does not mean the same thing across 15 languages (`async fn`, `suspend`, a goroutine, `Task<>`, an IO type). Decide whether the game wants **one uniform concept** — "this entity does something concurrent" — or per-language flavour. Uniform is almost certainly right for gameplay and much cheaper; per-language is a trap that grows with every grammar added.

- [ ] **rooms need more (and visually/behaviourally diverse) enemies.** Brainstormed 2026-08-19. The "raise hp per complexity and split into packs?" half is **already implemented, and separately measured not to be the lever** — so the open work is density and *diversity*.
  - **Already shipped, exactly as described.** `spawnEnemies` (`map/generation/enemies.ts:128`): a normal room gets `count = 1 + floor(complexity / COMPLEXITY_PER_EXTRA_ENEMY)` and `hp = complexity * HP_PER_COMPLEXITY / count` — HP scales with complexity *and* splits across a pack. An Elite room caps the budget at `ELITE_MAX_MEMBERS * ELITE_MEMBER_HP_CAP` (8 x 350) and splits that, flagging only the anchor.
  - **And redistributing HP is the lever class already shown not to move difficulty.** Three A/Bs left total damage taken invariant at **122/127/126**. Elites are ~5x deadlier per spawn and still only **11.6%** of damage on curl — 1,536 of them against 46,309 normals and 49,151 Edge Cases. The measured driver is **exposure-time x enemy count**. Judge any change here on *damage taken*, never on HP placed.
  - **DONE 2026-08-20 — density shipped as `COMPLEXITY_PER_EXTRA_ENEMY` 10 -> 5, and the old wording here was wrong about where the enemies are.** At 10 the constant was very nearly inert: real `complexityScore` is p50=**1**, p75=3, p90=6, p99=19 over 8,143 corpus entities, so **95.5% of entity rooms held exactly one enemy** (p99=2, max=4). "Lowering it thickens every normal room" was therefore false — it thickened almost nothing. At 5: +15.7% entity-room enemies, 14.3% of rooms affected, demo-campaign 320 -> 362 (+13.1%). Full numbers in `history.md`.
    - **A cap and a floor were both measured and rejected**: a cap is a no-op (cap 3 removes 0.2% of enemies, cap 4 removes 0.0% — there is no per-room tail), and a floor of 2 is +90.4% touching 95.5% of rooms. The 522-enemy laravel level is hundreds of *one-enemy* rooms plus corridor Edge Cases, not dense packs — so this constant was never that tail's lever and the frame-time caution above was aimed at the wrong knob.
  - **Diversity: half-answered, and the remaining half needs a fourth archetype.** A private/protected room now concentrates its pack HP in a heavier anchor (47.1% of multi-member packs), which is exactly DPS-neutral. But **archetype uniformity inside an entity room is still essentially unfixed**, and the reason is structural rather than a missing rule:
    - Only `normal` and `edgeCase` are available in an entity room — flagging a member `elite` breaks "one Elite per Elite room", which `stage-campaign.mjs` and every archetype report rely on. So **every archetype move is a DPS cut** (normal 4.21/s vs edgeCase 1.68/s) and there is no "tougher" one to reach for. HP shape was the only DPS-neutral axis left.
    - The switch-heavy skirmisher swap that did ship reaches **0.91% of enemies / 3.22% of entity rooms** — the honest ceiling, since `switchBranches > 0` is 4.7% of entities and most are single-enemy rooms. At a 4-member gate it fired on **1 enemy in 7,704**.
    - **The facts are language markers more than code markers**, which caps any fact-driven rule: private/protected is 33.8% overall but 10% (django) to 53% (ripgrep); `nestingDepth >= 2` 14.9% (and already triple-used by terrain); switch 4.7%; allocation-dense 2.0%; and **54.7% of entities carry no fact at all**.
    - So the real next step is **a fourth archetype**, not another rule over the existing three. Sized separately.
  - **DONE 2026-08-19 — the weapon concept shipped; what it did NOT buy is the interesting part.** `ENEMY_WEAPONS` (`combatConstants.ts`) gives each archetype its own speed, damage, cooldown window, inherent scatter and bolt colour, and `enemyAi.ts` picks by archetype. Full numbers in `history.md`.
    - **Mean ranged DPS per archetype is deliberately unchanged, exactly** — Elite doubles damage and doubles its cooldown, Edge Case halves both, and a test pins each archetype's `damage / meanCooldown` against the old model. So this moved *texture*, not damage taken, and it does **not** count as progress against the "exposure-time x enemy count" driver. Density and archetype *distribution* are still the untouched levers.
    - **The one field deliberately left flat is `radius`.** It feeds the hit test, so varying it moves effective DPS without touching either term the invariant compares. It is in the table with a test pinning the three values equal — spending that budget is a one-line change that must be argued for, not slipped in.
    - **`edgeCase` is still only spawned into corridor breakup rooms**, so a normal entity room remains uniformly one archetype. Giving the archetypes distinct weapons does nothing about that, and it is now the biggest remaining half of "diversity".
  - **The thematic direction, and it is nearly free.** Enemies currently read only `complexityScore`, but the parser already extracts `nestingDepth`, `visibility`, a switch/match summary, the line span and `kind` per entity — terrain already uses them (labyrinths, key-locked rooms, Switchboards) and enemies could too at **zero parser cost**: deep nesting -> keeps to cover, switch-heavy -> swaps behaviour between states, private/protected -> a guard archetype in locked rooms, long-but-simple -> slow high-HP "boilerplate", method vs free function -> different silhouette.
  - **What is *not* free**: recursion, call-graph fan-in, async, deprecation. These need new AST extraction — see the item below, which sizes it (my first estimate here, "~15 language adapters", over-stated it: the vocabulary is one merged table, and the real cost is elsewhere).
  - **Pre-register before touching it**: any enemy-scaling change moves `SIMULATION_BALANCE`, so `defaultHighscore.ts` must be regenerated *after* the design settles, not before (this cost two regenerations once already). Do not undo the Elite cap. And "the bot clears it" is not evidence for a human — the bot is superhuman at some things and far worse at others.

- [ ] rooms need deco of some kind, currently an empty wasteland — implemented in Task 21 (server racks/plants/desks/code-blocks as billboard props) but felt annoying in playtest, so disabled again behind `DECORATIONS_ENABLED = false` in mapGenerator.ts. **Root cause confirmed by the user (2026-07-16): it's specifically the billboarding** — decor objects rotate to always face the camera, which reads as visibly wrong/artificial for boxy furniture-like shapes (a rack/desk/block spinning to track your POV as you walk past it), unlike round/symmetric things where billboarding is unnoticeable. Not a placement-density or art-style problem — the rendering *technique* itself is the issue. Code (generation + rendering) is intact; re-enabling needs a genuinely non-billboard rendering approach, not just a re-tune. Ignore for now (kept open, see the technical path below).
    - [ ] deco objects from wad? — investigated (2026-07-16, no code changed yet), then **revised** once the user confirmed the real root cause above was billboarding, not visual style/placement — this changes the answer meaningfully from the original pass.
      - **Billboard sprites (WAD or procedural) don't fix this at all** — swapping the procedural shapes for real WAD sprite art would still be billboarded (same `projectPoint`/always-face-camera rendering), so it would still spin to face the player exactly like today's boxes do. Real WAD sprite loading (as scoped in the "enemy textures from wad?" note above) is a legitimate, separate feature, but it is **not** an answer to this specific complaint — correcting my earlier note's framing here.
      - **The actual fix needs non-billboard geometry — real, orientation-fixed solid objects.** The raycaster already has exactly this: `placePillars` (`src/map/generation/props.ts`) places real 1×1 solid wall tiles (`grid[y][x] = 1`) to break up large rooms, rendered through the normal wall-rendering path — genuinely 3D, correct from every angle, no billboard rotation, because it's real raycaster geometry rather than a camera-facing sprite. The raycaster's wall renderer (`raycaster.ts`) already dispatches texture-per-tile-*kind* (plain wall vs `DOOR_TILE` vs `SECRET_WALL_TILE` vs `LORE_TILE` each get distinct textures) — a new tile kind per `DecorKind` (e.g. a `RACK_TILE`) would fit that exact same dispatch mechanism, textured distinctly per kind.
      - **This reframes the WAD question entirely, and in decor's favor**: solid-geometry decorations would use the *wall*-texture pipeline, not the sprite-patch pipeline — meaning they'd reuse the WAD loading/allowlist mechanism that already exists and already works today (`loadWad.ts`/`textureAllowlist.ts`, the same system real walls/doors/floors already use), not the net-new sprite-loading work the enemy-textures note scoped. WAD-sourcing solid decor textures is comparatively *cheap* once the non-billboard rendering itself is built — genuinely just another texture slot + allowlist, same shape as the existing wall/door/floor ones.
      - **Collision trade-off: resolved, not a concern (user, 2026-07-16)**: solid geometry means real collision (like pillars), diverging from the current system's "decorations never block anything" design goal — user confirmed this is fine ("if i run into a room, and there is a desk and i run into it, i am not surprised to be blocked :D"), i.e. real-world-furniture collision reads as expected/intuitive, not an annoyance. Clears the way to design this as solid, colliding geometry from the start (pillar-like placement via the existing `findPropSpot` logic) rather than needing a walkthrough/non-blocking variant.
  - **Verified 2026-08-20: the solid-tile fix above only covers one of the four kinds.** A grid raycaster draws full-height columns by construction, and there is no partial-height wall path in `raycaster.ts`, so a `RACK_TILE` reaches the ceiling. Against the heights these props were actually designed at (`decorSizeFactor`, `sprites.ts`): `rack` 0.85 is plausible floor-to-ceiling, `block` 0.55 is marginal, `desk` 0.50 and `plant` 0.45 are not. And `plant` is round, so by the root cause above it never had the billboard defect at all and can go back on unchanged. **Shippable subset: `rack` as solid geometry, `plant` as a billboard, `desk`/`block` held.** The rest of this item re-checked clean — `DECORATIONS_ENABLED` (`props.ts:16`), `placePillars`, `findPropSpot`, the generation half, the billboard collector (`sprites.ts:757`) and the per-tile-kind texture dispatch (`raycaster.ts:383-417`) are all intact.
  - **`desk`/`block` need the same capability the windows item below prices**, and neither item noticed it needed the other despite being scoped in the same session: partial-height banded columns, which need a per-column span list instead of the scalar `zBuffer`. **The wall-fixtures item is *not* in this group** — it draws the fixture inside the wall texture, which is texture space rather than geometry, so it is unblocked.

- [ ] **objects on walls (properly sized, not simply "slap a texture on the whole wall")** — scoped 2026-08-19, now **posters and flat fixtures only**; lamps and windows are their own items below. This is the *wall-surface* half of decoration, the floor-standing half being the "rooms need deco" item above.
  - **The mechanism, shared with the deco item: a new tile kind plus a texture slot.** `raycaster.ts` already dispatches wall texture by tile kind (`hitTile === DOOR_TILE || BRANCH_DOOR_TILE : LORE_TILE : ...`), so a `POSTER_TILE` drops into that chain exactly as a `RACK_TILE` would for solid props. **And it is what "properly sized" asks for**: the fixture is drawn *within* the wall texture at correct proportions rather than stretched across it — precisely how DOOM does wall fixtures.
  - **One caveat to design around:** a tile kind textures *all four faces* of that block, so a poster would appear on every side. The renderer already distinguishes the hit side (it dims y-side walls to fake directional lighting), so per-face selection is reachable — but it has to be built deliberately, not assumed.
  - **Posters: showing what?** Source-derived is the thematically right answer and the cheap options are free — the file's own name/path, the entity's signature, the language. **Images from the source tree are cheap for a local workspace and currently impossible for a GitHub one**: `RemoteFileHandle.getFile()` returns `{ text(): Promise<string> }` and nothing else, so a repo-loaded workspace cannot supply bytes without extending that interface. Nothing decodes images today, though `createImageBitmap` needs no dependency. Needs a size cap and a rule for *which* images.

- [ ] **wall lamps / flares.** Split out 2026-08-19 from "objects on walls". **There is no lighting model to hang this on** — the renderer has distance fog plus a fixed dim on y-side walls to fake directionality, and nothing else. No point lights of any kind. So the sub-item is really two, with very different costs:
  - **Glow is cheap and worth doing.** An emissive fixture texture plus a local brightness boost, modulating the `fogBrightness` scalar the renderer *already computes per column* by proximity to the light. Arithmetic on a value that exists, in a pass that already runs.
  - **Shadows are a different renderer.** Real occlusion means testing every light against every column, inside the loop `perf-review-2026-08-02.md` measures at ~93% of frame time. Either decline it outright or scope it alone with a frame budget agreed *before* any code — this is the sub-item most likely to quietly eat the 60fps target.
  - Note the two are separable in the fiction as well as the code: a lamp that glows but casts no shadow reads fine, which is what most of this era's games actually did.

- [ ] **windows into the next room, with enemy aggro through them.** Split out 2026-08-19 from "objects on walls". **Constraint from the user: a window must not be a whole wall tile — nothing goes floor-to-ceiling.** That single requirement is what makes this much harder than "a see-through tile", and it is worth understanding before scoping:
  - **A grid raycaster draws full-height columns by construction.** A partial-height opening means one screen column has to be *banded* — near wall above, the far surface through the middle, near wall below — which needs **two DDA results per column** (near hit and whatever is behind it), two texture slices, and its own fog distance for the far slice. The DDA today stops at the first solid tile and has no compositing at all.
  - **And the depth buffer cannot express it.** `zBuffer` is a `Float64Array` indexed by column — **one depth per column** — and the sprite pass occludes with `proj.depth >= zBuffer[x]`. A banded column has two depths, so an enemy seen through the window is either wrongly hidden (near wall's depth stored) or wrongly drawn over the wall (far depth stored). Fixing it properly means a per-column *span* list rather than a scalar, which changes the sprite pass too. **This is the real cost of the feature, not the transparency.**
  - **The simulation half is smaller but genuinely new**: a window is **solid to bodies and bullets, transparent to sight** — a *third* category beyond the two `src/engine/mapPredicates.ts` has (`isWall` = solid to a body or a bullet, `isRouteBlocking` = solid to a route planner). The engine and the bot need it *differently*: an enemy should aggro through a window, while the bot must not try to shoot through one. That module already expresses exactly this kind of divergence as an argument (`sealedCorner`), so it is the right home.
  - **Then decide whether the player can shoot through.** If yes it is a firing port and changes tactics; if no, "I can see him but cannot hit him" has to be legible from the art alone.
  - **Cheaper things that get most of the feel**, worth pricing against the above before committing: a full-height barred opening (no banding needed, still a window fictionally), or a purely decorative lit alcove with no sight line at all.
  - **The span list is not windows-only (2026-08-20).** The same per-column span buffer is what `desk`/`block` in the "rooms need deco" item need in order to stop being floor-to-ceiling, so if this ever gets built it should be priced against both items rather than windows alone. The cost is in the fan-out, not the DDA: `zBuffer` has 94 occurrences across 6 non-test engine modules.

### Rendering, HUD & assets

- [ ] **rework hud to look like actual doom — SHIPPED 2026-08-20, and three of this item's premises were wrong.** The classic STBAR layout is in: `AMMO | STABIL | TOOLS | face | SWAP | KEYS | SCORE | ammo table`, with the settled shorthands. What stays open is only the WAD-art enhancement layer at the bottom of this item. Full numbers in `history.md`. Corrections, because they are why the work was smaller than scoped:
  - **"Not floating corner overlays, which is what `drawHud` draws today" was false.** `drawHud` already painted a full-width bottom bar (`fillRect(0, y0, w, HUD_HEIGHT)`), and `doc/user/hud-and-ui.md` already called it "The bottom status bar". The floating things are the minimap, FPS readout and toasts — different functions. So this was extending a bar, not replacing overlays with one.
  - **"Keys — the one that genuinely breaks" was stale.** `MAX_GATE_ROOMS = GATE_COLOR_COUNT = 4`; measured p50 2 / p90 3 / max 3. The 321-key era predates that cap. The existing pips needed no new idiom — they got a panel.
  - **"DOOM lists 4 types with current/max" does not port**: there is no ammo cap anywhere in this game. Nothing in `ammo.ts` or `lootApply.ts` clamps a pool and the only ceiling is IDKFA's. The table shows one number per row; inventing a maximum would have changed scores, and with them the shipped board and every replay.
  - **The real defect was one the item did not mention**: panel x positions were literals, so the bar never scaled — ~800px of dead space at the Sharp preset. Fixed by `hudLayout.ts`, a pure function tested at every preset.
  - **Still open, deliberately**: the bar is fixed-pixel and so is every other overlay, so at Sharp the whole overlay layer is half-size. Scaling the layer *as a whole* — HUD, minimap and viewmodel together — is its own item; scaling only the HUD would give it twice the weight of the weapon.
  - **Perf: measured, not argued.** Busy time is indistinguishable from master when the two are run back to back (branch 6.10-6.40ms against master 6.00-6.40ms). An earlier comparison against a baseline captured 2.5h before showed a false +0.45ms regression — the DVFS lesson again: a light-duty busy A/B needs its control in the same session.
  - **Panel by panel, against what this game actually has.** Three of the six map cleanly, three do not, and the gaps are the design work:
    - **AMMO / HEALTH** — fine. Both exist (`stats.health`, and the current weapon's pool via `ammoRemaining`).
    - **ARMS** — maps *better* than expected. DOOM shows 2-7 because slot 1 is fists/chainsaw; here `NUMBER_KEY_WEAPONS` filters out `meleeRange` weapons, giving **5 non-melee** for the grid and **2 melee** (knife, Toolchain) as the "slot 1" equivalent. Fills 5 of 6 boxes. **Bind the grid to `NUMBER_KEY_WEAPONS`, never to `WEAPONS` array order** — they differ, and `numberKeyCodeFor` already documents that trap.
    - **ARMOR — settled: the panel keeps DOOM's shape and this game's word.** There is no armor mechanic and none is being invented; **`SWAP` goes in that slot, labelled SWAP** (user, 2026-08-19). That is the rule for the whole bar: borrow the *layout*, keep our vocabulary — so the HEALTH panel reads `SYSTEM STABILITY`, not HEALTH, and the ammo rows use `AMMO_META`'s own names.
    - **Our words are longer than DOOM's, so they get shorthands** (user, 2026-08-19). DOOM's labels are 4-6 characters *by design* — that is what lets them sit under a big numeral in a fixed-width panel (`AMMO`/`HEALTH`/`ARMS`/`ARMOR`, and `BULL`/`SHEL`/`RCKT`/`CELL` in the table). **Settled** (user, 2026-08-19) — use these, do not re-litigate at draw time:

      | panel | our term | shorthand |
      |---|---|---|
      | AMMO | ammo | `AMMO` |
      | HEALTH | System Stability | `STABIL` |
      | ARMS | the weapons, which are dev tools | `TOOLS` |
      | ARMOR | Swap | `SWAP` |
      | table rows | bullets / shells / smg ammo / rockets / gas | `BULL` / `SHEL` / `SMG` / `RCKT` / `GAS` |

      `STAB` is deliberately not the health shorthand — four characters and it fits, but it reads as a melee verb in a shooter. `TOOLS` over `ARMS` because the weapons genuinely are dev tools and it costs nothing to say so. Both confirmed by the user.
    - **Put the shorthands in one shared place, not inline in `hud.ts`.** The ammo ones belong beside `AMMO_META`'s existing `label`, which the HUD already derives from (`label.toUpperCase()`, per its own comment) and the console log also uses — a `short` field there keeps the pairing that comment exists to protect. The panel labels can be named constants in `hud.ts`, but named, so a future rename moves one string.
    - **Keys — the one that genuinely breaks.** DOOM has exactly 3 colours x card/skull. This game generates a key per locked room, coloured by `gateColors.ts`, in a *variable* count (one bad parse produced 321). A fixed slot strip cannot represent it; needs a count, a scroll, or a different idiom.
    - **Ammo table** — DOOM lists 4 types with current/max. This game has **5 pools** (bullets, shells, smg, rockets, gas), and the shotgun additionally has a *magazine* on top of its pool, so "current / max" does not mean the same thing. Needs a 5th row and a decision about which number is which.
  - **The face is the one element needing new engine state.** `STFACE` reacts to health *and the direction damage came from*, and nothing tracks damage direction today. The user's reference swaps the avatar art anyway ("besides the player avatar lol"), so the art is free — the *behaviour* is an engine change and should be costed as one, or the face can simply react to health alone.
  - **Real DOOM art is available but must stay optional.** `STBAR`/`STTNUM*`/`STFACE*` are patch-format lumps and `patch.ts` decodes that format generically — no new binary parser, same finding as the enemy-sprites item. But it needs the player's WAD, so it can only be an enhancement layer: the bar has to look right with nothing loaded.
  - **Possible perf upside, to measure not assume.** `perf-review-2026-08-02.md` found the expensive class is "geometry Skia cannot emit as axis-aligned quads", and the HUD draws text and shapes every frame. A pre-composited bar blitted with one `drawImage` may be *cheaper* than what is there. Directly measurable — the HUD already has its own `?ablate=hud` group and `perf:bench` has scenarios.
  - **A full-width bezel costs viewport.** DOOM's bar eats ~20% of the screen; this renderer draws the 3D scene to a canvas sized independently of it. Check what shrinking the scene does to both feel and frame time before committing to the layout.
  - **Licensing, same split as the WAD-audio item**: the player's own WAD is settled precedent from textures; catalog WADs in `public/wads/` are served from the deployed site, so shipping their HUD art is redistribution — check `onlineWadCatalog.ts`'s bar for that case.

- [ ] enemy textures from wad? if yes: what about hitbox? — investigated (2026-07-16, no code changed yet).
  - **Today: enemies are 100% procedural, zero WAD sprite loading exists.** `src/wad/loadWad.ts`'s `WadLoadResult` only ever loads wall/door/floor-style textures (`wallTexture`/`doorTexture`/`floorTexture`/etc.) — no sprite lump is read at all. Enemies are drawn in `src/engine/sprites.ts` as solid-color rectangular billboards (`enemyColor`/`enemyBodyColor`), sized by a single `sizeFactor` in `projectEnemy` (`ENEMY_SIZE`, scaled by `EDGE_CASE_SCALE`=0.55 or `ELITE_SCALE`).
  - **Loading real sprite art is feasible, same binary format already parsed**: Doom sprite lumps use the exact same "patch" pixel format as wall patches (`src/wad/patch.ts`'s `parsePatch` is already format-generic, not wall-specific) — no new binary parser needed. But the pipeline differs from walls: sprites are used as single patches per frame directly (no `compositeTexture.ts`-style multi-patch tiling/`TEXTURE1` step), and use Doom's frame+rotation lump-naming convention (e.g. `TROOA1`, `TROOA2A8` mirrored) that nothing in this codebase currently parses. This project's WAD-texture system also has a real security/curation stance worth respecting here, not shortcutting: `src/wad/textureAllowlist.ts` is a hand-curated, individually-IWAD-verified name list per slot (not "trust any name present") — real monster sprites (imp/zombieman/demon/etc.) would need the same treatment: a new, separately-verified allowlist, not a generic passthrough.
  - **The hitbox question is the real design fork, and it's grounded in something concrete**: today, hitbox and visual are *literally the same rectangle* by construction — `projectEnemy`'s one `sizeFactor`-derived projection is the exact object both `enemyBodyColor`'s fill (drawing) and `findTargetInProjections` (hit test, used by both ranged fire and melee's `meleeWouldHit`) operate on. Real Doom sprite art has irregular silhouettes with substantial transparent padding inside its bounding box — reusing today's full-rect hit test unchanged against real sprite pixels would make every enemy's hitbox visibly more generous than its drawn silhouette, a real fairness/feel regression, not just a cosmetic swap.
  - **Follow-up investigated (2026-07-16): what real silhouette-accurate hitboxes would actually take.** Turns out meaningfully cheaper than "pixel-alpha-accurate hit-test project" implies — the pieces already fit together well:
    - **The opacity data is already there for free.** A Doom patch's post format (`src/wad/patch.ts`) *is* its own alpha channel — each column's posts are exactly its runs of opaque pixels; any row not covered by a post in that column is transparent by definition. No separate alpha lookup or new data structure needed once a sprite's `Patch` is loaded — `patch.columns[x]` already answers "is this pixel opaque" directly.
    - **The engine's hit-test geometry is already a real 2D screen rect, not a raycaster-column hack.** `projectPoint` (`sprites.ts`) returns genuine `left/right/top/bottom` screen coordinates from one `size` value — `findTargetInProjections` checks a single `screenX` column against `cy` (the screen's fixed vertical center, since this engine has no mouselook pitch — `cy` is always `height/2`). That means mapping a hit-test point into the sprite's *native* pixel space is one clean linear interpolation per axis: `patchCol = floor((screenX - proj.left) / (proj.right - proj.left) * patch.width)`, `patchRow = floor((cy - proj.top) / (proj.bottom - proj.top) * patch.height)` — then look up `patch.columns[patchCol]` for a post covering `patchRow`. Elite/Edge-Case scaling doesn't complicate this at all: `proj`'s rect is already scaled, so the interpolation is unaffected by which scale factor produced it.
    - **The real new requirement is frame/rotation state, not the math.** Hit-testing needs the *exact same* patch (frame + rotation) that was actually drawn to the player that tick — today's system gets this for free by construction (draw and hit-test share the literal same rect object); a sprite-based version needs an explicit "current displayed patch" resolved once per enemy per tick and threaded to both the draw call and the hit test, or the two could silently diverge (a shot that visually missed the silhouette registering as a hit, or vice versa) — a real correctness requirement, not just an implementation detail.
    - **Blast radius**: `findTargetInProjections` is shared by ranged fire *and* melee's `meleeWouldHit` — a silhouette-accurate hit test would change melee timing/feel too unless deliberately scoped to ranged-only (an explicit decision to make, not an accident to discover later).
    - **Performance is a non-issue**: one column lookup + a short post-list scan per hit test (O(small) — a sprite has few posts per column) — negligible even for a multi-pellet shotgun blast checking several enemies at once.
  - **Revised recommendation**: given the above, real silhouette hitboxes are a legitimately achievable *second phase* once sprite loading itself exists (previous bullet) — not the much harder ask it first looked like. Still recommend shipping cosmetic-only sprites first (rectangular hitbox unchanged, zero risk to existing weapon-accuracy tuning) and treating silhouette-accurate hit-testing as a deliberate, separately-scoped follow-up, specifically because of the frame/rotation-sync requirement above — that's real new state to get right, worth its own focused pass and verification rather than bundling into the initial sprite-loading work.
  - **Real scope, if picked up**: new sprite-lump loading path in `loadWad.ts` (single-patch, no composite step), a new curated+IWAD-verified monster-name allowlist (same rigor as the existing wall-texture one), a decision on how many frames/rotations to support (Doom sprites can have up to 8 rotations plus walk/attack/death sequences — even a reduced subset is real per-enemy-kind work, not a one-line flip), and — if/when the silhouette-hitbox follow-up is picked up too — the frame-sync mechanism described above plus an explicit ranged-vs-melee scoping decision.

- [ ] **sounds/bgm from wad (local bgm would override it)** — checked 2026-08-19, and the answer to "is that even possible" is **yes for sounds, effectively no for classic music**. Split it.
  - **Sound effects are easy, and the plumbing already exists.** DOOM SFX are `DS*` lumps in about the simplest format there is: a short header (format id, sample rate — usually 11025 — and sample count) followed by **unsigned 8-bit PCM**. Decoding is a `DataView` read plus `(b - 128) / 128` into an `AudioBuffer`, the same style `patch.ts` and `flatLump.ts` already use, and `parseLumpDirectory` (`wadFile.ts`) already hands out arbitrary lumps — no new plumbing. `audio.ts` already routes everything through an SFX bus, so sample playback sits beside the procedural path and inherits the volume sliders for free.
  - **Music is the hard half, and it is a dependency problem rather than a format one.** DOOM music is `D_*` lumps in **MUS**, a compact MIDI-like event stream. MUS to MIDI is a well-documented, easy conversion — and then you are holding MIDI, which is *notes, not audio*. Browsers have no synthesizer, so playing it needs either a SoundFont player (FluidSynth-in-WASM, megabytes) or an OPL3 emulator to sound like the real thing. That is a very large dependency for a project that hand-rolled a ZIP reader rather than take `unzipper` — see [Dependency Minimalism](doc/dev/decisions.md#dependency-minimalism).
  - **But some WADs ship real audio and those are free.** Doom II BFG and many PWADs replace MUS with MP3/OGG lumps, which `bgm.ts`'s existing `<audio>` + `MediaElementAudioSourceNode` path already plays. So "music from WAD" is worth shipping *for WADs that contain actual audio files* and worth declining for classic MUS — detect and skip, do not half-build a synth.
  - **The real design work is the mapping, not the decoding.** DOOM's sound names are DOOM's game: `DSPISTOL`, `DSSHOTGN`, `DSSGCOCK`. This one has 27 named events including `playAcidOverflow`, `playUltraKill`, `playKeyPing`, `playTeleport`, and weapons (gdb, ghidra, Friday Hotfix, Toolchain) with no DOOM equivalent at all. So it needs a curated mapping table with a per-event fallback — the same shape and the same rigour as `textureAllowlist.ts`, which already requires every name to decode against a real WAD.
  - **Procedural stays the default and the fallback.** The existing SFX are a deliberate design, not a placeholder; a WAD is an *override*, and any event without a mapping must fall through to the procedural sound rather than going silent.
  - **Precedence, per the original note**: local BGM folder > WAD music > silence. `bgm.ts` is already a playlist provider, so a WAD source is a second provider at lower priority rather than a rewrite.
  - **Two smaller traps**: `audio.ts` creates its `AudioContext` lazily on the first user gesture (autoplay policy), so lump decoding has to happen after that or be deferred; and catalog WADs from `public/wads/` are *served from the deployed site*, so enabling this for them is redistributing audio rather than reading the user's own file — check `onlineWadCatalog.ts`'s licensing bar for that case specifically, even though the user's-own-WAD case is already settled precedent from textures.

- [ ] User reports drastic framedrops when looking around or shooting on the nightmare github level suggestion (magento/magento2). I can't repro these locally. He uses Chrome on Win10 and the game usually runs at 120fps, but when shooting - even when out of ammo! - his fps drop to around 30. It's not an hw topic, since he has a Ryzen9, plenty of ram and a RTX3090. Confirmed still reproducing for this user even after the Task 82-85 engine perf pass (see the "code optimization/reuse" and magento entries in Done above) — that fix did not resolve it, reopened on purpose. Still unresolved/unrepro'd, but added opt-in diagnostics to capture from *his* machine next time it happens — see `src/engine/perfDebug.ts` (`?perfDebug=1`): per-frame phase-timing breakdown (input/sim/raycast/billboards+targeting/firing/particle-effects/hud), an "unaccounted" ms figure (raw frame time minus everything we measured — the tell for a GC/compositor/OS-level stall outside our own code), live entity counts, mousemove-event rate (tests a high-poll-rate-gaming-mouse-flooding-the-handler theory), Chrome heap usage, and audio shot-count/AudioContext state. Confirmed via code reading that the projection cost Tasks 82-85 removed doesn't run at all on an out-of-ammo `fire()` call (`ammo < ammoPerShot` returns before touching `projectLivingEnemies`/`audio.playShoot`), so whatever's causing the "even out of ammo" drop is something else this instrumentation should surface. Logs via `console.log` (NOT `console.debug` as previously noted here — corrected 2026-08-16; perfDebug.ts's own comment explains the lines are deliberately short and rate-limited because they DO mirror into the console sidebar). Next step once it fires again: send him a `?perfDebug=1` link, have him keep DevTools open with "Verbose" enabled, and get the captured log back. **2026-08 frame-budget audit context: his "120fps normally" means a 120Hz display — an 8.33ms frame budget, against which the pre-audit render busy time (~7.7ms, 93% of it the floor-cast + wall pass, measured scene-independent) leaves essentially zero margin; any GC/compositor/OS hiccup halves his rate to 60/30. That arithmetic explains "drops even when out of ammo" without any per-shot cost. Shipped: u32 floor-cast (-0.5ms). The real 120Hz enabler is the half-res floor option (-3.5ms, busy ~4.4ms = 53% of his budget), parked with the settings-tab item below. Not reproducible locally at 60Hz: quiescent captures show zero drops in every scenario incl. s4-magento-fire (perf_runs 2026-08-15/16); no 120Hz panel here to validate directly.**

- [ ] **DELAYED UNTIL WE LEAVE BETA (user, 2026-08-10).** fog of war for minimap, same as automap (never applies to compass, always points to exit)

### Multiplayer & backend

The multiplayer *features* are still one-liners in the last section; this is the operational half.

- [ ] **Self-updating multiplayer backend: `docker/update.sh --install` / `--uninstall` sets up a systemd timer.** Decided 2026-08-19 (user) after checking the alternatives — see [decisions.md](doc/dev/decisions.md#the-backend-updates-itself-by-pulling-ci-never-pushes-to-it).
  - **Nothing in CI changes, and no marker file or version endpoint is needed.** The git remote *is* the channel: the timer runs `update.sh`, which already `git pull`s and rebuilds. The original sketch's `.update-backend` + inotify cannot work (the frontend deploys by FTPS to a *webhost*; the backend is a different machine, no shared filesystem) and is dropped.
  - **The update logic already exists — do not reimplement it.** `update.sh` aborts on a dirty tree, detects the TURN profile from the running containers, pulls `coturn` only when relevant, rebuilds/recreates, prints status, warns on `docker/.env.example` drift. `--install`/`--uninstall` only need to write/remove a `.service` + `.timer` and reload; both must be idempotent.
  - **The one real wrinkle is sudo without a TTY.** `update.sh` deliberately runs as the *deploy user* and shells to `sudo` only for the docker calls, because a root `git pull` leaves the repo root-owned. Under systemd there is no TTY to prompt on, so this needs a `NOPASSWD` sudoers entry scoped to those compose commands. `--install` should write it (or verify it and fail loudly), because discovering it via a silently failing 04:00 timer is the worst version of finding out.
  - **Cadence: a quiet hour, not hourly.** Restarting signaling drops **open lobbies and pending joins** — `sessions` is an in-memory `Map` with a 5-minute TTL, so it is ephemeral by design, but someone sitting in a lobby gets kicked. Established peer connections survive, since peers talk directly once the handshake is done. So: `OnCalendar` at a quiet local hour, `RandomizedDelaySec`, and `Persistent=true` so a run missed while the host was off fires at next boot.
  - **Make failure visible.** A timer that silently stops is the classic form of this bug — the dirty-tree guard aborting loudly only helps if someone sees it. Add an `OnFailure=` unit, or at minimum have `--install` print the `systemctl status` / `journalctl -u` invocations and document them in `multiplayer-deployment.md` §6.6 next to the manual line this replaces.

### Platform, tooling & tests

- [ ] **Add more repo hosts beside GitHub — GitLab and Codeberg SHIPPED 2026-08-20; Bitbucket still open.** Narrowed 2026-08-19: the original wording was "should support any http(s) available git repo", and that half is **out of scope by decision** (see [decisions.md](doc/dev/decisions.md#repo-hosts-are-api-adapters-not-a-git-client)). This item is now only the API-backed hosts.
  - **The abstraction is already right, so this is genuinely small.** `RemoteFileHandle` (`workspace.ts:19`) is a *single* method — `getFile(): Promise<{text(): Promise<string>}>` — and everything downstream (file tree UI, `flattenParsableFiles`, entrypoint detection, level launching, replay) only reads `kind`/`name`/`path`/`children`. `github.ts` is 274 lines doing exactly two things: one recursive tree call (`/repos/{o}/{r}/git/trees/{branch}?recursive=1`) and lazy per-file text from `raw.githubusercontent.com`. **Nothing downstream changes.**
  - **Per host**: a sibling of `github.ts` (recursive tree endpoint + raw-file endpoint), a URL/shorthand parser (`parseGithubRepoInput` is github.com-only by regex), a `#tab-*` entry in `index.html`, and tests.
  - **Do not skip the rate-limit error path.** The GitHub one exists because unauthenticated is **60 req/hr**, and `raw.githubusercontent.com` does not CORS-expose its rate-limit headers — so a 403 there has to be *inferred* rather than read. Every host will need its own equivalent, and getting it wrong shows up as an unexplained "failed to fetch".
  - **Check CORS on the actual host before writing the adapter**, not after. It is the only property that decides whether a host is possible at all, and self-hosted Gitea/Forgejo instances configure it per-deployment — so "Gitea works" is a claim about an instance, not about Gitea.
  - The deeplink item below says "github/demo" and needs the same generalisation.
  - **Shipped 2026-08-20: GitLab and Codeberg, behind one auto-detecting "Repo" tab** (the GitHub tab renamed; paste any URL and the host is read off the domain, bare `owner/repo` still means GitHub). The abstraction held exactly as this item predicted — `RemoteFileHandle` never changed and nothing downstream did either. What it did **not** predict: Full write-up in [history.md](doc/dev/history.md).
    - **Both new hosts paginate the recursive tree at 100 entries; GitHub does not.** The single biggest structural difference, and it forces a cap: `gitlab-org/gitlab-foss` is 86,540 entries — **866 pages** — against an unauthenticated budget of **500 requests/hour**, so fetching one repo whole would spend the hour and still come up short. Capped at 40 pages, reusing the existing `tree.truncated` flag and the marker `renderFileTree` already draws from it.
    - **GitLab's rate-limit headers are not CORS-exposed.** It sends `ratelimit-remaining`/`ratelimit-reset`, but its `Access-Control-Expose-Headers` list omits them, so a header-gated check is dead code from a page — exactly the trap `github.ts` documents for `raw.githubusercontent.com`. Status alone is the signal. (`x-next-page` *is* exposed.)
    - **GitLab's raw-file path must be `%2F`-encoded.** The obvious `/-/raw/<path>` URL sends no CORS header at all, and the unencoded API path 404s. Verified against the live host rather than assumed, because the failure looks like a missing file.
    - **Nested groups made GitLab's parser swallow other hosts' URLs.** Supporting `group/subgroup/project` means accepting any number of segments, so it read `codeberg.org/owner/repo` as a project in a group called `codeberg.org/owner` — and it is registered first, so it won. Caught by a UI test, not by review.
    - **The truncation marker had GitHub hardcoded in it**, so a capped GitLab tree would have told the player GitHub did it. Host-neutral now.
  - **Bitbucket is the remaining half and is unproven.** Its API answered `ACAO: *` on the probe, but the probe itself 404'd, so nothing about the tree shape or its pagination has actually been checked. Redo the CORS-and-shape pass against a repo that exists before scoping it.

- [ ] end2end/functional/visual tests (coverage target: all aspects of gameplay), and a dedicated audit confirming **no data leaves the app at all**. That claim used to read "besides highscores", which was forward-looking; public highscore boards were scoped and **scrapped** on 2026-08-19 (see [decisions.md](doc/dev/decisions.md#highscores-stay-on-the-device)), so the assertion is now the simpler and stronger one: `highscores.ts` is `localStorage` only and there is no outbound user data anywhere in the product. Assert it as a hard invariant — a `fetch` to anything but the signaling server (multiplayer only, and opt-in by joining) is a regression. The unit-test half of this item (100% coverage) shipped as Task 92 — see `## Done`. `scripts/verify-demo-campaign.mjs` (structural/map-generation coverage) and `scripts/verify-campaign-playthrough.mjs` (a real headless-Chromium save/highscore/replay mechanism test) are existing starting points to build the broader e2e/visual coverage on top of.

- [ ] **threaded / forked tests to speed them up? — the premise is already satisfied; what is left is elsewhere.** Checked 2026-08-19, because the item predates both halves being solved by other means.
  - **The unit suite is already forked and parallel.** `vitest.config.ts` sets no `pool` or `fileParallelism`, so vitest 4's defaults apply — forks, one worker per core (16 here). Measured: `vitest run --dir src` is **40.8s wall for 3,127 tests** against **69.8s** of summed worker time, and that gap *is* the parallelism. `--dir scripts` is 0.5s for 586. There is no win left to take here.
  - **The wall clock is the 16 browser-driven `verify-*.mjs` scripts**, which no vitest pool setting can reach — they are separate Node processes each driving a real Chromium/Firefox/WebKit. CI already parallelises them the only way available, by *job*: `verify-browser` is a 3-browser matrix, and `verify-replay` (45min timeout), `verify-multiplayer-transition` (20min), `verify-multiplayer-multiguest` and `verify-determinism` were each split into their own job on 2026-07-22 precisely so their multi-minute cost runs concurrently instead of stacking onto a shared chromium leg.
  - **What is actually left, in order of annoyance:**
    - **`npm run coverage` cannot be read locally while `balancing_corpus/` exists** (98MB, present after `npm run balancing:corpus`). Vitest's default glob picks up ~39 vendored QUnit/Django spec files and reports them as failures, so the gate is CI-only in practice and every local run needs `--dir src` instead. A `test.exclude` for the corpus closes it and is the single biggest local-DX win in this item.
    - **CI runs `npm run coverage || npm run coverage`** (`verify.yml:79`) — the whole ~81s suite twice on any flake. Find out what the retry was papering over before removing it; it may be load-bearing.
    - `verify:multiplayer-campaign` is deliberately *not* CI-wired (no bounded worst case) and is not a candidate.
  - **Before switching `pool` to `threads`**, note the suite constructs real `RaycasterEngine`s and jsdom canvases and relies on `restoreMocks` plus per-file module isolation, which `forks` gives for free — measure it, do not assume it is a drop-in.

